/* ===========================================================================
 * Game Boy Emulation tab, area 3 of eight: Memory, timer, interrupts, transfers, serial, pad
 * gbMemoryEmulationTabHTML(d) returns this area's HTML; the tab's composer in
 * gb-emulation-tab.js calls every area in order (2 November 2026: the tab
 * was split into area files so a builder can read one area at a time).
 * Loaded by a plain <script src> tag (the site must open over file://).
 * =========================================================================== */

function gbMemoryEmulationTabHTML(d) {
  return '<div class="gbemu-area" id="gbemu-area-memory">' +

  '<section class="sect gbemu-area-head" id="gbemu-area-memory-head"><h3 class="sect-h">Area 3 of eight: Memory, timer, interrupts, transfers, serial, pad</h3>' +
    '<p class="gbemu-key">The timer and divider, the sprite-memory transfer and its bus conflict, the scan during a transfer, the sprite-memory corruption bug, the interrupt line, and appendix A: every register bit by bit, the pad, the serial port, the five interrupt vectors, the display block and the read-back OR table. HALT, STOP and the dispatch are instructions, so they are in the processor area (appendix D).</p>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable">This area is the file <code>gb-memory-emulation-tab.js</code>, one of the eight area files that together make the Game Boy Emulation tab. It can be read on its own; the build checklist is in the first area, and the rule for what a build must implement is beside it.</p>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable"><b>Where the rest of this page is.</b> When a sentence here points to a section or appendix that is not in this area, it is in one of these files (the links work on the assembled page):</p><ul class="gbemu-bullets"><li><b>Overview, build checklist and interface</b> (<code>gb-machine-emulation-tab.js</code>): <a href="#gbe-m1">1. Clocks, map, and one phase that matters</a> <a href="#gbe-m3">3. Where the machine starts</a> <a href="#gbe-gaps">24. What this page does not cover</a></li><li><b>Processor</b> (<code>gb-cpu-emulation-tab.js</code>): <a href="#gbe-m2">2. The processor</a> <a href="#gbe-ops">Appendix D. The instruction set</a></li><li><b>Picture</b> (<code>gb-ppu-emulation-tab.js</code>): <a href="#gbe-m5">6. The sprite scan</a> <a href="#gbe-1">7. The decision that decides everything</a> <a href="#gbe-2">8. Step the machine by dots</a> <a href="#gbe-3">9. The fetcher</a> <a href="#gbe-4">10. The two FIFOs</a> <a href="#gbe-5">11. The window is a separate machine</a> <a href="#gbe-6">12. Sprites stall the pipeline</a> <a href="#gbe-7">13. Writes that land mid-line</a> <a href="#gbe-8">14. Interrupts, blocking, power</a> <a href="#gbe-addr">Appendix C. Tile, map and object addressing</a> <a href="#gbe-pipe">Appendix E. The picture pipeline, in numbers</a></li><li><b>Sound</b> (<code>gb-apu-emulation-tab.js</code>): <a href="#gbe-s1">15. The sound unit is not on the processor&rsquo;s clock</a> <a href="#gbe-s2">16. The square channels</a> <a href="#gbe-s3">17. Wave, noise, and power</a> <a href="#gbe-sound-regs">Appendix B. The sound registers, and the primitives the refinements modify</a></li><li><b>Cartridges and mappers</b> (<code>gb-mappers-emulation-tab.js</code>): <a href="#gbe-mappers">Appendix H. The cartridge mappers, register by register</a></li><li><b>Super Game Boy and link devices</b> (<code>gb-sgb-emulation-tab.js</code>): <a href="#gbe-sgb">Appendix A, continued: the Super Game Boy and the link-port devices</a></li><li><b>Tests and scores</b> (<code>gb-acceptance-emulation-tab.js</code>): <a href="#gbe-9">18. Traps that cost us weeks</a> <a href="#gbe-10">19. What to test with, in order</a> <a href="#gbe-11">20. How to measure a change</a> <a href="#gbe-score">21. The score, and what a score is worth</a> <a href="#gbe-unwinnable">22. Rows nobody can win honestly</a> <a href="#gbe-12">23. The evidence</a> <a href="#gbe-labels">Appendix F. Labels, flexibility, and what is not established</a> <a href="#gbe-verify">Appendix G. The verification scorecard</a></li></ul>' +
  '</section>' +
  '<div class="gbemu-toc"><b>In this area</b><a href="#gbe-m4">4. Timer, DMA, interrupt line</a><a href="#gbe-hw">5. What a real Game Boy settled</a><a href="#gbe-regs">Appendix A. Every register, every bit</a></div>' +

  '<section class="sect" id="gbe-m4"><h3 class="sect-h">4. Timer, DMA, interrupt line</h3>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable"><b>Units in this section.</b> Timer and divider counts are in T-cycles; a machine cycle is four T-cycles. Machine cycles in the transfer table are counted from W, the machine cycle that carries the write to <code>0xFF46</code>. Dots are on section 14&rsquo;s <b>access scale</b> and are counted from A, the dot on which that write&rsquo;s access is made.</p>' +
    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">The timer is one counter, read two ways</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-1"><b>Rule 4/1.</b> The timer and the divider must be a single <b>16-bit counter advancing by 1 every T-cycle</b>. The divider register (<code>FF04</code>) reads the counter&rsquo;s top eight bits (bits 15 to 8). A write of any value to the divider <b>zeroes the whole counter</b>, low bits included, not only the visible byte.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Because the write zeroes the low bits too, a divider write can itself produce a timer tick (Rule 4/2).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-2"><b>Rule 4/2.</b> The counting register (TIMA, <code>FF05</code>) must increment by 1 on each <b>falling edge</b> (1 to 0) of <code>(counter bit N) AND (timer enabled)</code>, where N is <b>9, 3, 5, 7</b> for the four clock-select settings (TAC bits 1&ndash;0 = <code>00</code>, <code>01</code>, <code>10</code>, <code>11</code>; appendix A.1) and &ldquo;timer enabled&rdquo; is TAC bit 2. The edge is taken on the AND, not on the counter bit: every change that takes the AND from 1 to 0 is one increment, whether the counter bit falls as the counter advances, a TAC write clears the enable while the bit is 1, or a divider write (Rule 4/1) zeroes the counter while the bit and the enable are both 1.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> TAC = <code>0x05</code> (enabled, select <code>01</code>, so N = 3) and the counter stands at <code>0x0008</code>: bit 3 is 1, the AND is 1. (a) A write of TAC = <code>0x01</code> clears the enable; the AND falls from 1 to 0 and TIMA increments once. (b) Instead, a write to the divider sets the counter to 0; bit 3 falls, the AND falls, and TIMA increments once. (c) With the counter at <code>0x0010</code> (bit 3 is 0), the same TAC write changes nothing: the AND was already 0, so there is no edge.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Falling edge of the AND, not of the bit: toggling the enable can manufacture an edge, and that glitch is a real test.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-3"><b>Rule 4/3.</b> <b>Overflow is two events with different timing, and this is the crux.</b> When the counting register increments from <code>0xFF</code>, in this order:' +
      '<ol class="gbemu-steps">' +
        '<li>On that T-cycle (the overflowing tick) the counting register becomes <code>0x00</code> and the timer request (IF bit 2) is set. The interrupt request is the carry itself, so the request bit is set <em>at</em> the overflow cycle.</li>' +
        '<li>The counting register holds <code>0x00</code> for the next 4 T-cycles. A falling edge of Rule 4/2 before the copy&rsquo;s own T-cycle (a TAC or divider write, or the counter bit) still increments it, to <code>0x01</code>, and the copy still lands and overwrites that.</li>' +
        '<li><b>4 T-cycles after the overflowing tick</b>, the modulo register (TMA, <code>FF06</code>) is copied into the counting register. A falling edge of Rule 4/2 on this same T-cycle is applied after the copy, so the register then holds TMA + 1 (with TMA at <code>0xFF</code>, a new overflow).</li>' +
      '</ol>' +
      'The <b>overflow machine cycle</b> is the machine cycle that contains the overflowing tick. The <b>reload machine cycle</b> is the whole of the next machine cycle. The copy of step 3 always falls inside the reload cycle&rsquo;s four timer ticks, before that cycle&rsquo;s processor access, whichever of the four ticks overflowed. So a read of the counting register in the overflow cycle returns <code>0x00</code> (the register reads <code>0x00</code> for one machine cycle), and a read in the reload cycle returns the copied modulo (TMA + 1 when step 3&rsquo;s same-tick edge lands after the copy).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> The four timer ticks of a machine cycle come before its access (E.5). The counting register overflows on the second timer tick of machine cycle M. IF bit 2 is set on that tick; a read in M returns <code>0x00</code>. The copy lands on the second timer tick of M+1, 4 T-cycles later and before M+1&rsquo;s access, so a read in M+1 returns TMA. Had the overflow come on the fourth tick of M, the copy would land on the fourth tick of M+1: still before M+1&rsquo;s access.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> MECHANISM, read from our source (<code>tima_reload_delay</code>, <code>tima_reloaded</code>). Set the request at the reload instead (four T-cycles late) and an overflow produced by an enable glitch dispatches one instruction too late.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-4"><b>Rule 4/4.</b> <b>Three writes behave abnormally, not two, and the window you are in decides which.</b> <b>The two windows are different windows</b>: the <em>overflow</em> machine cycle, in which the carry has fired and the modulo has not yet been copied, and the <em>reload</em> machine cycle one later, in which it is copied (Rule 4/3).' +
      '<ul class="gbemu-bullets">' +
        '<li><b>Counting register, written in the overflow cycle: the write is KEPT, and it cancels the reload.</b> The delayed copy never happens and the written value stands.</li>' +
        '<li><b>Counting register, written in the reload cycle: the write is DISCARDED</b> and the modulo wins.</li>' +
        '<li><b>Modulo register, written in the reload cycle: it lands in both</b>: the modulo takes the value and so does the counting register.</li>' +
      '</ul></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> TMA holds <code>0x80</code> and the counting register overflows in machine cycle M. (a) A write of <code>0x40</code> to TIMA in M: TIMA reads <code>0x40</code> from then on and TMA is never copied. (b) A write of <code>0x40</code> to TIMA in M+1: the write is lost and TIMA reads <code>0x80</code>. (c) A write of <code>0x20</code> to TMA in M+1: TMA and TIMA both read <code>0x20</code>. In all three cases IF bit 2 was set in M (Rule 4/5).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> The overflow-cycle write is the arm an implementer hits first, because a program that services the timer interrupt promptly writes exactly here.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-5"><b>Rule 4/5.</b> The request was set at the carry, a machine cycle before the reload, so <b>a write that aborts the reload does not abort the interrupt</b>: the request bit stays set and the handler still runs.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why, and where the published account and this page place the same event differently.</b> <b>Published accounts that put the request at the reload get a fourth arm out of this (a write in the overflow cycle suppressing the interrupt too) and that arm is not expressible here and is not ours to claim.</b> <b>The placement is settled on the board, not by preference:</b> all thirteen <code>acceptance/timer</code> rows pass with the request at the carry, <code>tima_write_reloading</code> and <code>tma_write_reloading</code> among them, and the reload placement is refuted by name (<code>rapid_toggle</code> fails on it, because an overflow manufactured by an enable glitch then dispatches one instruction late). What is honestly open is narrower and is stated as such: <b>nothing we own distinguishes the two placements on the abort question</b>, because no cartridge on the board writes the counting register inside the overflow cycle with the interrupt enabled. <b>NOT ESTABLISHED</b> for the interrupt arm; the reload arm is <b>read from source</b>: <code>src/gb.c</code>, the <code>0xFF05</code> write case, which sets the value and zeroes the reload delay whenever the reload has not already landed. <b>Falsifier:</b> a cartridge that overflows with the timer interrupt enabled, writes the counting register inside the overflow cycle, and reports whether the handler ran.</div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">Sprite-memory transfer</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-6"><b>Rule 4/6.</b> <b>One byte per machine cycle, 160 bytes, after a start-up of two machine cycles.</b> Number the machine cycle in which the write to <code>0xFF46</code> lands W, and the dot its access is made on A (the access dot scale is in section 14). In every machine cycle, reads and writes alike, the transfer takes its step <b>before</b> the processor&rsquo;s access in that cycle. The machine cycles then run as this table says:' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Machine cycle (counted from W, the cycle of the write)</th><th>The transfer&rsquo;s step (before the access)</th><th>A processor access to sprite memory in that cycle</th><th>Dot of that access (access scale, counted from A)</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td>W (the write)</td><td>nothing for the new transfer; a transfer already running moves its next byte</td><td>as before the write: open if no transfer was running, locked if one was</td><td>A</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>W+1</td><td>start-up: nothing moves</td><td>open, unless a transfer was already running</td><td>A+4</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>W+2</td><td>the transfer becomes active; nothing moves</td><td><b>locked</b>: reads return <code>0xFF</code>, writes are dropped</td><td>A+8</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>W+3+j, for j = 0 to 158</td><td>copies byte j</td><td>locked</td><td>A+12+4j</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>W+162</td><td>copies byte 159 and ends</td><td><b>open</b>, with all 160 bytes in place</td><td>A+648</td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-7"><b>Rule 4/7.</b> <b>What the table implies, stated so nobody has to derive it.</b> The processor is locked out of sprite memory for exactly 160 accesses, W+2 to W+161, and the lock leads the byte writes by one machine cycle. The picture processor sees the transfer active from dot A+8 (the origin the grip lag of Rule 5/5 is counted from) and byte j from dot A+12+4j.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> With the write&rsquo;s access on dot A, the picture processor sees byte 0 from dot A+12, byte 1 from A+16, and byte 159 from A+12+4&times;159 = A+648, which is W+162&rsquo;s dot in the table. A processor read of sprite memory on dot A+8 (W+2) returns <code>0xFF</code>; one on dot A+648 (W+162) returns the stored byte.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-8"><b>Rule 4/8.</b> A second write to <code>0xFF46</code> during a running transfer (a restart): the old transfer moves its byte in the write&rsquo;s own cycle, nothing moves in W+1 and W+2, the lock never lifts, and the new byte 0 lands in W+3. The picture processor&rsquo;s &ldquo;transfer active&rdquo; (the state its grip history records, Rule 5/5) also never drops through a restart: it stays set through W+1 and W+2, so the grip is held without a gap.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Read from our source (a restart re-arms the start-up and leaves the active flag set).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-9"><b>Rule 4/9.</b> A processor read on the transfer&rsquo;s bus (section 5&rsquo;s conflict rule, Rule 5/9) returns source byte 0 in W+2 and source byte j in W+3+j (the byte the transfer reads in that cycle). So the conflict, like the lock, covers W+2 to W+161 (j runs to 158); in W+162 the transfer has ended before the access and the bus answers normally. In W+1 of a restart (Rule 4/8) the running transfer&rsquo;s conflict is already on the new source&rsquo;s group, and a read there returns the new source&rsquo;s byte c &minus; 1 (byte 0 when c is 0), where c is the number of bytes the old transfer has copied, the one it moved in the write&rsquo;s own cycle included.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why, and the labels.</b> MECHANISM, read from our source: <code>DMA_END_LATE</code> 0. The whole table and Rules 4/7 to 4/9 are MECHANISM, read from our source and INSTRUMENT-MEASURED on a probe of the shipped core. On our core the start-up is exact, not a representative. Measured on the 66 original-machine Mooneye acceptance cartridges without a boot program: start-up 2, step before the access, passes all 65 that do not need the boot program, every transfer row among them (<code>oam_dma_start</code>, <code>oam_dma_restart</code>, <code>oam_dma_timing</code>, <code>oam_dma/basic</code>, <code>oam_dma/reg_read</code>, and the thirteen instruction-timing rows that time an access against a running transfer). Start-ups of 1 and 3 each fail 16 of them and 4 fails 18. An arrangement in which, in write cycles only, the transfer takes its step after the processor&rsquo;s access gives a different picture, measured on our core: 2 fails <code>oam_dma_start</code> and eleven other rows, 3 fails it and four others, so no integer satisfies everything and a half-cycle grid seems to be needed. With the step before the access in every cycle, 2 passes all of them and no half-cycle grid is needed.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-10"><b>Rule 4/10.</b> <b>A second whole-cycle structure is equivalent to ours, and a build may use either.</b> Lock the processor out from W+2, read source byte j in W+2+j and write it in W+3+j. Against ours the only difference is the cycle in which a source byte is read, and the processor cannot change a source byte in between: before W+2 both read after the processor&rsquo;s write, and from W+2 a processor write to the source&rsquo;s bus is dropped (section 5). So the two agree on every byte written to sprite memory and on everything the picture can see. The one thing they would not agree on is a conflicting processor read on the transfer&rsquo;s bus: <b>that read follows Rule 4/9 whichever structure is used</b> (byte 0 in W+2 and in W+3, byte j in W+3+j).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> MECHANISM, argued from the two structures; an independent build with that structure passed all 20 transfer-probed rows (the 20 of 20 was measured on that independent build).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why the start-up is load-bearing: it is the origin of the grid the grip phase is measured against.</b> Section 5 specifies when the transfer takes the bus as a <em>dot-resolved lag</em> (Rule 5/5), and a lag is relative to something. It is relative to the instant the transfer becomes active, which is the instant the start-up expires. <b>An implementer who does not place the start-up cannot place the grip</b>, and the grip is what eleven of the 35 transfer cartridges turn on. State the start-up first, then the lag on top of it.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-11"><b>Rule 4/11.</b> While it runs (W+2 to W+161) the transfer <b>owns sprite memory</b>, <b>and only sprite memory</b>: processor reads there return <code>0xFF</code> and processor writes there are dropped, while the rest of the bus stays reachable, subject only to the bus conflict of Rule 5/9 on the bus the transfer occupies.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> This is exactly what the instruction-timing tests observe, and it is why transfer routines run from high RAM. Block more than sprite memory and those tests hang: section 5 has the measurement and the rule that replaces it (Rules 5/8 and 5/9).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-12"><b>Rule 4/12.</b> The transfer&rsquo;s own reads of its source are never blocked: a source in video memory is read directly even while the line is drawn (the mode-3 block is the processor&rsquo;s).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Read from our source.</div>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable"><b>&ldquo;The transfer owns sprite memory&rdquo; is a statement about the processor, and it is not the rule the object scan obeys.</b> The scan does not skip objects while a transfer runs; it <b>files all ten of them on coordinates it did not re-read</b> (Rule 5/4). That is a different shape and it is the difference between one board row and none: section 5, HARDWARE-OBSERVED on 35 cartridges. <b>And it is two rules, not one:</b> the scan-latch behaviour (Rule 5/4), and the <em>phase</em> at which the transfer takes the bus (Rule 5/5). Build the first alone and eleven of those cartridges stay red.</p>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-13"><b>Rule 4/13.</b> A source address at or above <code>0xE000</code> <b>mirrors work RAM</b> on the original Game Boy: <b>exactly, source address A reads work memory at A &minus; 0x2000</b> (the low 13 bits, so the whole range <code>E000</code>-<code>FFFF</code>, pages <code>FE</code> and <code>FF</code> included, reads <code>C000</code>-<code>DFFF</code>; nothing in <code>FE00</code>-<code>FFFF</code> itself is read). <b>Our core does the same on the Colour</b>, with the upper 4 KiB always bank 1 (that row is unresolved, so the original&rsquo;s value is returned).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> A write of <code>0xFE</code> to <code>0xFF46</code> copies <code>DE00</code>-<code>DE9F</code> into sprite memory; a write of <code>0xFF</code> copies <code>DF00</code>-<code>DF9F</code>; a write of <code>0xE0</code> copies <code>C000</code>-<code>C09F</code>. Here the letter A is a source address, not the access dot of Rule 4/6.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Read from our source. (Published accounts say a Color reads <code>0xFF</code> instead, and the single test that pins the original&rsquo;s behaviour is declared as failing on Color, so it provides no coverage there at all.) That test is Mooneye&rsquo;s <code>oam_dma/sources-GS</code>, and it disables cartridge RAM before it checks <code>0xE0</code>, <code>0xFE</code> and <code>0xFF</code>, so what it pins is that the <em>internal</em> work RAM answers even though the transfer&rsquo;s own address decoder is asserting the cartridge-RAM select on the external bus at the same moment. Both halves of that are in GB-CTR&rsquo;s table 8.1 and its warning beside the register, and the collision is the reason the warning exists: a flash cart that answers there is driving the same bus the work RAM is.</div>' +

    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-4-14"><b>Rule 4/14.</b> The status interrupt is <b>one line with four sources ORed together, firing only on that line&rsquo;s rising edge</b>. STAT bits 6, 5, 4 and 3 each <em>enable</em> one source (the table in A.3); the enabled conditions are ORed into a single internal line, recomputed every dot, and the request bit at <code>FF0F</code>&nbsp;bit&nbsp;1 is set when that line goes from 0 to 1, and at no other time. Section 14 has the rest.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> The coincidence source (bit 6) and the mode-0 source (bit 3) are both enabled. On some line the coincidence condition becomes true while the mode is 2: the line goes from 0 to 1 and the request bit is set. The coincidence condition is still true when mode 0 begins on that line, so the line is already 1: mode 0 makes no rising edge and no second request. Had the coincidence condition gone false before mode 0 began, the line would have fallen to 0 and mode 0 would raise a second request.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Write it as literally that and recompute it every dot; the much-discussed &ldquo;blocking&rdquo; behaviour is then not a feature you implement, it is what the expression does by itself when two enabled conditions overlap. Everything called &ldquo;STAT blocking&rdquo; is what that one sentence does by itself: it is not a rule to add. <b>MECHANISM</b>, and section 14 is the long version, including the case where a level comparator must <em>not</em> raise twice.</div>' +
  '</section>' +


  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- HW
   * The real-hardware section. Everything in it comes from our own
   * DMG, read on 16 September 2026 through an EverDrive GB, plus the two
   * cartridge-declared results that sit alongside it. Each claim carries its
   * label inline -- DERIVED / HARDWARE-OBSERVED / CARTRIDGE-DECLARED / NOT
   * PINNED -- because the whole value of the section is that the distinction
   * is visible. Do not promote any of them by editing the label out.        */
  '<section class="sect" id="gbe-hw"><h3 class="sect-h">5. What a real Game Boy settled</h3>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable">The rules in this section were settled on an original Game Boy. The evidence: blargg&rsquo;s <code>oam_bug</code> cartridges run on an original Game Boy, from an EverDrive GB, with each cartridge&rsquo;s own result buffer brought back (the save file it writes, not a photograph of the screen), and the other stations of the bench table below. They are here because the documents everyone builds from do not contain them: Pan Docs, GB-CTR and Kevin Horton&rsquo;s VRAM timing note were each opened at the exact place the answer would live, and each is silent, wrong, or literally marked <code>TODO</code>. Every claim below says which, and says what kind of claim it is.</p>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable"><b>Units in this section.</b> A machine cycle is four dots. <code>dots_into_the_line</code> and every dot of the corruption rules count the line&rsquo;s dot counter from 0 at the line&rsquo;s first dot. Grip dots count from the first dot on which the picture processor sees the transfer active (dot A+8 of Rule 4/6&rsquo;s table). W and A are Rule 4/6&rsquo;s.</p>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">The bench, its denominator, and the stations that produced nothing</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>The denominator for every hardware row on this page: one original Game Boy (a DMG, the grey brick, not a Color) and one EverDrive GB flash cartridge.</b> Not two consoles, not two cartridge readers. Where a claim below is marked HARDWARE-OBSERVED, <b>n&nbsp;=&nbsp;1 console</b> unless it says otherwise.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Three qualifications the record does not close, stated as open rather than assumed.</b> <b>The console&rsquo;s board revision is not recorded</b>: DMG revisions are known to disagree with each other on picture-processor behaviour elsewhere in this project, by hundreds of pixels on some tests, so every finding below is <em>one revision, unnamed</em>, and a reader whose console disagrees has not necessarily found a defect here. <b>The panel is not recorded in words but it is visible in the captures</b>: an original grey body with its original bezel and a reflective, non-backlit screen, no aftermarket panel. An observation from the photographs and not a recorded fact, and it matters only for the stations read by eye, because an aftermarket panel changes contrast and can add or remove apparent ghosting. And <b>no station was repeated on a second console.</b></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>The stations.</b> Nine stations were set up. <b>Four produced no capture at all, and two more produced a capture nobody has read or a capture that says the program did not run.</b> That is stated here rather than discovered at the bottom, because the negative space is part of the evidence: <b>only stations 01, 04 and 09 settled anything on silicon.</b> Station 06 is recorded as one that <em>could not have</em> settled its question rather than one that failed to: see the methodological residue under Rule 5/7, which is the most reusable thing in this section.' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Station (#)</th><th>Question</th><th>Instrument</th><th>Capture</th><th>State</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td>01</td><td>The sprite-memory corruption bug: which row, and what is written</td><td>blargg <code>oam_bug</code> 7 and 8</td><td>save files, decoded</td><td><b>SETTLED</b>: Rules 5/1 to 5/3</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>02</td><td>The noise channel&rsquo;s shift-register width, changed mid-run</td><td>authored, 3 cartridges</td><td>3 audio recordings</td><td><b>CAPTURED, NEVER ANALYSED</b></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>03</td><td>Does the frequency sweep write back after an overflow</td><td>3 cartridges</td><td><b>none: nothing captured</b></td><td><b>NOT MEASURED</b></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>04</td><td>With a sprite-memory transfer covering the scan, what survives</td><td>ashiepaws <code>strikethrough</code></td><td>photographs</td><td><b>SETTLED</b>: see Rule 5/4</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><b>09</b></td><td>Does the scan-latch account hold on silicon, at every transfer alignment</td><td>35 self-checking cartridges, another project&rsquo;s</td><td>35 photographs</td><td><b>SETTLED</b>: 35 of 35 PASS; the label and its limits are under Rule 5/4</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>05</td><td>Which expression replaces the refuted corruption expressions</td><td>authored <code>oamfill.gb</code></td><td><b>none: nothing captured</b></td><td><b>BUILT, NOT RUN</b></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>06</td><td>Which object survives, at every alignment</td><td>authored <code>dmashift.gb</code></td><td>six blank frames; cartridge rebuilt</td><td><b>NOT A NULL</b>: the program did not run</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>07</td><td>Wave and square trigger phase (Color only)</td><td>authored <code>trigphase.gb</code></td><td><b>none</b></td><td><b>BUILT, NOT RUN</b></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>08</td><td>Noise-to-PCM tie (Color only)</td><td>authored <code>noisetie.gb</code></td><td><b>none</b></td><td><b>BUILT, NOT RUN</b></td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>And the standing rule behind every label on this page: a measurement without a stated denominator is not evidence.</b> &ldquo;Six of six identical&rdquo; is empty until the denominator is named. Where a finding below rests on one console, one fill pattern or one photograph, it says so on the claim and not in a footnote. <b>And where you may build your own measurement:</b> several of these were reached with a particular cartridge on a particular bench, and a developer in another language may reach the same place with a different instrument. Where that is true, the finding states <b>the invariant your instrument must satisfy</b>, not our numbers.</div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">The sprite-memory corruption bug: the row is derivable</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-1"><b>Rule 5/1.</b> On the original Game Boy, a 16-bit increment or decrement of a pointer whose value before the operation is inside <code>0xFE00&ndash;0xFEFF</code> (<code>inc</code>/<code>dec rr</code>, and <code>push</code>&rsquo;s implied <code>dec sp</code>), or a read or write through such a pointer, corrupts a row of sprite memory (Rules 5/2 and 5/3). A read or write counts whichever way its address was formed: a direct address (<code>LD A,(a16)</code>, <code>LD (a16),A</code>) and an opcode or operand fetch with PC in that range corrupt as reads and writes do; PC&rsquo;s own increment raises no event (Rule D.11/1). Rows are eight bytes. The corruption happens only when all of these hold at the moment the event is raised: the device is an original Game Boy; the screen is on (LCDC bit 7 = 1); the line is below 144; <b>the line&rsquo;s dot counter, as it stands when the event is raised, is below 80</b> (an increment or decrement reads it before its machine cycle&rsquo;s four dots are processed, a bus access after them, as in Rule 5/2&rsquo;s first two steps; no 4 is subtracted for this test); <b>no sprite-memory transfer is active</b> (active as in Rule 4/6&rsquo;s table: from W+2&rsquo;s step to W+162&rsquo;s); and the address is in <code>0xFE00&ndash;0xFEFF</code>. If any one fails, nothing is corrupted.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Note the transfer clause: <b>while a transfer owns sprite memory the corruption does not happen.</b> And note that the window is the dot count, <em>not</em> &ldquo;the mode register reads 2&rdquo;: at the line&rsquo;s first machine cycle our own mode register still reports 0, so a gate on &ldquo;mode is 2&rdquo; misses that timing outright. Settled from our source (<code>oam_bug_live</code>).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-2"><b>Rule 5/2.</b> The row an event corrupts is <b><code>row = (dots_into_the_line &gt;&gt; 2) + 1</code></b>, where <code>dots_into_the_line</code> is the picture&rsquo;s dot counter at the <em>start</em> of the machine cycle that carries the event, for a bus access and for an increment alike. Take it as follows:' +
      '<ol class="gbemu-steps">' +
        '<li>For an increment or decrement: read the dot counter before that machine cycle&rsquo;s four dots are processed.</li>' +
        '<li>For a bus access (a read or a write, <code>push</code>&rsquo;s implied <code>dec sp</code> included): read the dot counter after the cycle&rsquo;s four dots are processed, and subtract 4.</li>' +
        '<li>When D.10.1&rsquo;s look has already run two dots ahead of a cycle, the counter is taken <b>as it stands</b>, those two dots included, not the nominal start of the cycle.</li>' +
        '<li>If the value of step 2 is below 0 (the cycle began on the line before), nothing is corrupted.</li>' +
      '</ol>' +
      'On section 14&rsquo;s access scale a cycle that starts with the counter at D makes its access on dot D + 4, so a bus access on access dot D is charged to the row of D &minus; 4, and an access on dot 1 belongs to a cycle that began on the line before and corrupts nothing.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> blargg&rsquo;s <code>7-timing_effect</code> timings <code>0x02</code>, <code>0x03</code>, &hellip;, <code>0x10</code> are machine cycles that start at dots 1, 5, 9, &hellip;, 57 of the line (the counter at the <em>start</em> of each cycle, not the access dot). Timing <code>0x02</code>: (1 &gt;&gt; 2) + 1 = row 1. Timing <code>0x03</code>: (5 &gt;&gt; 2) + 1 = row 2. Timing <code>0x10</code>: (57 &gt;&gt; 2) + 1 = row 15. So every timing t corrupts row t &minus; 1. Timing <code>0x02</code>&rsquo;s corrupting access, if it were a bus access, would land on access dot 5, and 5 &minus; 4 = 1 gives the same row 1. The row is never 0 (Rule 5/3 then does nothing for row 0 anyway).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why, and the evidence.</b> <code>7-timing_effect</code> walks the corrupting access one machine cycle at a time along the line and dumps sprite memory after each. <b>Fifteen clean timings came back</b>, <code>0x02</code> through <code>0x10</code>, and every one corrupts row <code>timing &minus; 1</code>: <code>0x02</code> hits row 1, <code>0x03</code> row 2, and so on to row 15. (The capture stops at <code>0x11</code> because the cartridge&rsquo;s own 8,188-byte result buffer filled, not because the session failed: the console runs past it too, so re-running it truncates identically.) The rule reproduces all fifteen, <b>including the line&rsquo;s very first machine cycle</b>, which is the one timing no bias on a scan counter can reach. Read from our source: both samples read the live counter; our source samples an increment before its cycle&rsquo;s tick and takes the cycle&rsquo;s four dots off a bus access sampled after it (<code>OAM_BUG_BUSPHASE</code>, MECHANISM); the dot-below-80 test of Rule 5/1 reads the counter as it stands when the event is raised. Read as mechanism, the row hit is <b>the one the picture processor is about to read next</b>, which makes row 0 unreachable and therefore <em>derives</em> the familiar exemption that objects 0 and 1 are never affected, instead of hard-coding it as a guard. <b>DERIVED.</b></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why one row per machine cycle.</b> The same reading confirms one more thing on silicon, independently of any document. In <code>8-instr_effect</code> the base row steps by exactly 4 between two particular pairs of sub-tests and by nothing elsewhere, and those two are the only sub-tests entered through one extra <code>ret</code>, which is 4 machine cycles. So <b>the picture processor advances one sprite-memory row per machine cycle</b>, arrived at from the cartridge&rsquo;s own structure rather than taken on trust.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>If you build your own instrument, the invariant it must satisfy:</b> sweep one corrupting increment across the first 80 dots of a line, one machine cycle per pass, and <b>the row index you recover must advance by exactly one per machine cycle and must reach row 1 at the line&rsquo;s first machine cycle.</b> Any row rule that cannot produce row 1 at dot 1 is wrong, however well it fits the middle of the sweep. You do not need our cartridge.</div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">The three documented corruption expressions are wrong</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-3"><b>Rule 5/3.</b> What a corruption at row r writes is <b>a plain copy from the preceding row</b>. Number the rows 0 to 19 and, within a row, the four 16-bit words 0 to 3; <code>W(r, w)</code> is word <i>w</i> of row <i>r</i>, little-endian in memory. <code>copy_row(dst, src)</code> copies all four words of row src into row dst; <code>copy_tail(dst, src)</code> copies words 1, 2 and 3 of row src into row dst and leaves word 0 of dst alone. A real access copies <b>the whole row</b>, before the access itself reads or writes. The increment-only glitch copies <b>the last three words and leaves the first untouched</b>. A read that carries its own increment puts <b>the accessed row&rsquo;s first word into the preceding row</b>, then copies that whole preceding row into <em>both</em> neighbours, so rows <code>r&minus;2</code>, <code>r&minus;1</code> and <code>r</code> come out byte-identical. Written so it can be built:' +
    '<div class="gbemu-pre">INCREMENT OR DECREMENT ONLY   (inc/dec rr with rr in $FE00-$FEFF,\n' +
    '                               and push&rsquo;s implied dec sp)\n' +
    '    if r &lt;= 0 or r &gt;= 20: nothing happens\n' +
    '    copy_tail(r, r-1)                  # words 1,2,3 only -- first word left ALONE\n\n' +
    'A REAL READ, OR A REAL WRITE\n' +
    '    if r &lt;= 0 or r &gt;= 20: nothing happens\n' +
    '    copy_row(r, r-1)                   # all four words\n\n' +
    'A READ IN THE SAME MACHINE CYCLE AS AN INCREMENT   (ld a,(hl+) / (hl-))\n' +
    '    # WIDER immunity than the other two: skipped unless 4 &lt;= r &lt;= 18\n' +
    '    if 4 &lt;= r &lt;= 18:\n' +
    '        W(r-1, 0) = W(r, 0)            # the accessed row&rsquo;s first word\n' +
    '        copy_row(r,   r-1)             # then the preceding row goes\n' +
    '        copy_row(r-2, r-1)             #   into BOTH neighbours\n' +
    '    # ...and the read itself then goes through the ordinary read path\n' +
    '    # above. Do NOT apply it twice -- that is what the two-part published\n' +
    '    # wording is distinguishing, and double-counting it is a known failure.</div>' +
      'The ordinary read path of the last case applies whether or not the <code>4 &lt;= r &lt;= 18</code> part ran.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> (a) <code>inc de</code> with DE in <code>0xFE00&ndash;0xFEFF</code>, charged to row 5: words 1 to 3 of row 5 become words 1 to 3 of row 4; word 0 of row 5 is unchanged. (b) <code>pop bc</code> whose read is charged to row 5: all four words of row 5 become row 4&rsquo;s. (c) <code>ld a,(hl+)</code> at row 6: word 0 of row 5 takes word 0 of row 6; then row 6 and row 4 both take all of row 5, so rows 4, 5 and 6 are identical; then the read&rsquo;s own ordinary corruption applies (row 6 takes row 5 again, which changes nothing further). (d) <code>ld a,(hl+)</code> at row 3: the first part is skipped (3 is below 4), and only the ordinary read corruption applies: row 3 takes all of row 2.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why: the bindings of the published expressions, defined here because a refutation that uses letters it never bound is uncheckable.</b> For an access at row <i>r</i> (a read, a write, or an increment/decrement only) the published expressions bind <code>a = W(r, 0)</code> (the first word of the row being accessed), <code>b = W(r&minus;1, 0)</code> (the first word of the <em>preceding</em> row) and <code>c = W(r&minus;1, 2)</code> (the <em>third</em> word of the preceding row). <b>For a read that carries its own increment the published expression binds four different words and writes to a different row</b>. The row <em>preceding</em> the accessed one: <code>A = W(r&minus;2, 0)</code>, <code>B = W(r&minus;1, 0)</code> (the word that gets corrupted), <code>C = W(r, 0)</code> (the first word of the <em>accessed</em> row) and <code>D = W(r&minus;1, 2)</code>. <b>These are not the same letters in both groups, and folding them together is a known way to get this wrong.</b> Keep the two cases written out separately in your code.' +
    '<div class="gbemu-pre">write corruption        W(r,0)   =  ((a ^ c) &amp; (b ^ c)) ^ c\n' +
    'read corruption         W(r,0)   =  b | (a &amp; c)\n' +
    'read during increment   W(r-1,0) =  (B &amp; (A | C | D)) | (A &amp; C &amp; D)</div>' +
      'All three also copy the row&rsquo;s <b>last three words</b> from the preceding row&rsquo;s last three words. Evaluated with those bindings and compared with 21 dumps, all three are refuted, and not degenerately: the increment case was checked on all 19 rows it touches:' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>The documented expression gives</th><th>This Game Boy gives</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td>Increment or decrement only<br><code>inc de</code></td><td><code>b</code></td><td><b><code>a</code>: the word is unchanged</b></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>A real read or write<br><code>pop bc</code>, <code>push bc</code></td><td><code>a</code></td><td><b><code>b</code>: the preceding row&rsquo;s word</b></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>A read during an increment<br><code>ld a,(hl+)</code></td><td><code>B</code></td><td><b><code>C</code>: the accessed row&rsquo;s word</b></td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table>' +
      '<b>Mind the letter group in that table&rsquo;s third row: it is the easiest slip in this section to make.</b> The read-during-increment case is bound in <b>uppercase</b>: <code>C = W(r, 0)</code> is the accessed row&rsquo;s first word, which is what that cell means, while lowercase <code>c</code> is <code>W(r&minus;1, 2)</code> (the <em>third</em> word of the <em>preceding</em> row, a different word entirely). The first two rows are the lowercase group. <b>Reading that third row in lowercase is exactly &ldquo;a known way to get this wrong&rdquo;.</b> The copy rule of Rule 5/3 reproduces all 21 dumps, and it is shorter than any of the expressions it replaces.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>HARDWARE-OBSERVED, ONE FILL PATTERN, ONE CONSOLE, not derived, and the qualification belongs on the claim rather than under it.</b> blargg fills sprite memory with byte&nbsp;=&nbsp;offset, and on that fill a copy is indistinguishable from any expression that happens to agree with it there. What is settled is that the documented expressions are wrong. What replaces them is what one fill pattern can see. Closing it needs a single capture with a different fill, and that needs an authored cartridge, because blargg&rsquo;s own cartridge chooses the fill. That cartridge exists and has not been run (station 05). It fills sprite memory from a generator seeded at <b>174</b>: the first seed of 200,000 searched whose fill makes <em>every</em> candidate rule give a different answer at every row it uses, and which also forces neighbouring rows&rsquo; tails to differ so the three-word copy is visible at all. <b>The search was over fills and never over rules</b>: the candidate expressions were frozen at the top of the generator before a byte was flashed. Under blargg&rsquo;s own fill the measured collision rate is <b>72 of 159 rival comparisons</b> (at one row, &ldquo;copy&rdquo; and both documented expressions all return the same word), which is the number that says why a second fill is needed rather than a second opinion.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>You do not need seed 174, and the invariant your fill must satisfy is the useful part:</b> at every row your cartridge reports, <b>the candidate rules must all return different words</b>, and <b>adjacent rows&rsquo; words 1 to 3 must differ</b> so that a three-word copy is visible at all. Search over fills; freeze the rules first. Any fill with that property answers the question, and your seed will not be ours. Until such a capture exists (ours does not; station 05 has captured nothing), <b>the copy rule keeps its one-fill-pattern label and must not be promoted.</b></div>' +


    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">Objects during a transfer: what the scan does, and two questions that are not questions</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-4"><b>Rule 5/4.</b> <b>The rule is not about hiding objects. It is about what the scan does not read.</b> The object-memory data path is a pair of byte-wide latches (one for the even byte, one for the odd) and <b>those latches are shared with the mode-3 tile fetch</b>. While a transfer holds the bus (from the grip of Rule 5/5), <b>the mode-2 scan performs no read into the latches</b> (Rule 5/7: the tile number and attribute are still taken past them), and the latches retain their previous contents. Everything downstream keeps running on those stale values: the comparator and the enqueue logic are not gated, so <b>every one of the forty slots tests against the same held Y</b> and all forty get the same answer. Where that held Y passes for the line, <b>all forty pass, and the ten-object cap fills with indices 0 to 9</b>: all ten stacked at <em>one</em> position, the one latched by the previous line&rsquo;s last clean read. The mode-3 fetch of each of the ten then takes its tile number and attribute <b>from the transfer</b> rather than from the object&rsquo;s own memory (Rule 5/6).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> In ashiepaws&rsquo; <code>strikethrough</code> a transfer covers the whole of the scan on line 68, so all forty objects are scanned while it runs. The reference (confirmed by a photograph of a real console) shows the text with <b>exactly one 8-pixel bar struck through it, at columns 71 to 78, and no other bar anywhere on the line</b>. Hide every object and you are wrong by 7 px; hide none and you are wrong by 49. Under Rule 5/4, ten identical objects are enqueued at the held position. <b>The visible bar exists because there are ten fetches:</b> the sequence of ten is what walks the transfer&rsquo;s cursor onto a byte pair that happens to be opaque. One bar, at one position, from ten objects that are all the same object.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why, and two questions that are not questions.</b> Pan Docs says that when a sprite-memory transfer is running during the scan, <i>&ldquo;most PPU revisions read each object as being off-screen and thus hidden on that line&rdquo;</i>. <code>strikethrough</code> refutes it (the example above). <b>The obvious next step is to say &ldquo;that one bar is OAM index 7&rdquo; and then ask which of the ten objects survives. Both halves of that are wrong, and it is worth seeing why, because the reasoning fails in a way that is easy to repeat.</b> <b>First: &ldquo;OAM index 7&rdquo; cannot be measured from that picture.</b> It can only be read off a pixel position that <em>two</em> objects share. That cartridge spaces its objects 8 apart from a base of 23, so the X byte wraps at 256 and <b>object 7 and object 39 land on exactly the same eight pixels</b>. The photograph cannot tell them apart. <b>Second: &ldquo;which index survives&rdquo; is not observable at all.</b> Two independent codebases produce <em>identical pixels</em> on this cartridge while attributing the surviving bar to <b>different indices (2 in one and 7 in the other</b>) purely as an artefact of their internal fetch order. <b>A question that two correct implementations answer differently while agreeing on every pixel is not a question about the hardware.</b> It is the wrong question, and the six rival rules registered against it are refuted with it.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>There is no fitted constant in that account.</b> Note what it does to the question of which object survives: no rule selects <em>one of ten</em>, because <b>all ten are enqueued and all ten are identical</b> (the single bar is a property of the fetch, not of a survivor).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>LABEL: HARDWARE-OBSERVED, on silicon.</b> The account itself is read from two competitors&rsquo; source, not derived here and not measured here; what was measured here is that a console agrees with it. <b>Denominator: 35 self-checking cartridges, expected answers baked in by their own author, run on one original DMG, 35 of 35 passing, photographed one file per cartridge.</b> The cartridges report on screen with no debugger (horizontal bands PASS, vertical bars FAIL) and the reading is scripted rather than eyeballed, on two checks that must agree (a directional brightness test across the crop, and the capture orientation that maps an image axis to a console axis), with the stripe count carried as corroboration and explicitly not as the rule. <b>The denominator stated so nobody inflates it: 35 cartridges, ONE console, ONE session, ONE operator. A single DMG settles a single DMG</b>, revisions are known to disagree, and nothing here compares two units. That limit is not retired by the run coming back clean. <b>Falsifier:</b> the same console re-run showing a fail pattern on any of the 35, a second DMG disagreeing on any of them, or any photograph turning out to be of a cartridge other than the one it is named after.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>And here is the half a later reader will get wrong, so it is stated before anything else is claimed: the hardware run upgraded the BEHAVIOUR and did not touch the MECHANISM.</b> A console agreeing with a rule tells you the rule holds on that console. It does not tell you what in the silicon makes it hold. The temptation after a clean hardware sheet is to write the whole account up as derived; this page refuses, and the refusal has a shape worth copying.</div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">The asymmetry inside that rule: one half is derivable, the other is asserted, and the split <em>is</em> the finding</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Do not flatten this into &ldquo;both were read from source&rdquo; and do not flatten it into &ldquo;both are derived&rdquo;.</b> Rule 5/4 has two halves and they do not have the same standing, in either of the two codebases that carry them or here.' +
      '<ul class="gbemu-bullets">' +
        '<li><b>The mode-3 address substitution: DERIVABLE.</b> One of the two implementations states a <b>shared address-latch model</b>: a single address latch, a per-device request mask, and one rule (<i>a requester writes the address only if no writer&rsquo;s request is pending</i>) applied uniformly to the processor, the increment unit and the picture processor. It is not a special case written for this test; its author generalised it empirically in a later commit that deletes two of his own TODOs, which is the strongest available evidence that the rule is load-bearing rather than fitted. <b>Given that model, the mode-3 fetch reading the transfer&rsquo;s address falls out.</b> It is a reading of somebody else&rsquo;s stated model and <b>it does not become ours because a console agreed with it.</b></li>' +
        '<li><b>The mode-2 read suppression: ASSERTED IN BOTH, MECHANISM UNKNOWN.</b> Neither author argues it. In one it is <b>a bare conditional at one of two call sites</b>, with no comment on the conditional, sitting under a <b>2018</b> comment that reads <i>&ldquo;It appears that DMA blocks PPU access to OAM, but it needs verification.&rdquo;</i>: eight years later it still says <em>needs verification</em>. In the other it is a picture-processor-layer guard keyed off <b>different state on a different timescale</b> than the address substitution above; mode 3 sits in the same acquired state and reads anyway. <b>The two halves are not one rule in that codebase either.</b></li>' +
      '</ul>' +
      '<b>Record it as: observed, heavily measured, mechanism unknown.</b> The behaviour is pinned by 32 self-checking cartridges across 16 phase alignments in one codebase and by 35 on silicon here: <b>a great deal of evidence <em>that</em> it happens and none at all about <em>why</em>.</b> <b>NOT ESTABLISHED, and what would establish it:</b> a structural account that <em>predicts</em> the suppression from a stated model. Anyone who later writes a derivation must show the structure, not restate the behaviour. <b>This is the exact place a label drifts if nobody guards it.</b></div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">The grip phase, <em>when</em> the transfer takes the bus. Ours, measured, and recorded as an invariant rather than a value</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-5"><b>Rule 5/5.</b> The scan&rsquo;s read suppression of Rule 5/4 (the grip) must follow the transfer&rsquo;s active state with a lag of L dots, where L is 1 or 2. <b>The lag is counted from the instant the transfer becomes active, which is two machine cycles after the write to <code>0xFF46</code></b>: dot A+8 of section 4&rsquo;s table, where the transfer becomes active in W+2&rsquo;s step, before W+2&rsquo;s access. The two statements name one instant. In steps, once per dot:' +
      '<ol class="gbemu-steps">' +
        '<li>At the end of each dot, record whether the transfer is active on that dot (the per-dot history, <code>grip_hist</code>).</li>' +
        '<li>A scan read on dot D is suppressed if and only if the record for dot D &minus; L says active.</li>' +
      '</ol>' +
      '<b>The lag applies at both edges</b>: the suppression begins L dots after the first dot on which the transfer is active, and ends L dots after the first dot on which the transfer is no longer active, not on that dot. <b>Our core ships 2</b> (<code>OAM_GRIP_LAG</code>). <b>The invariant, which is the recorded form of this rule and its flexibility clause:</b> <b>the scan slot in flight when the transfer takes the bus still completes its read, and the grip is in force before the next transfer machine cycle.</b> 0 and &ge;3 are refuted; 1 and 2 are indistinguishable on everything we hold.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example (L = 2).</b> The transfer is first active on dot A+8. A scan read on dot A+9 checks the record of dot A+7 (not active) and reads; a scan read on dot A+10 checks dot A+8 (active) and does not read. At the end, if dot E is the first dot on which the transfer is no longer active, a scan read on dot E+1 checks dot E&minus;1 (active) and is suppressed; one on dot E+2 checks dot E (not active) and reads. With L = 1 every one of these dots moves one earlier.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why, and how it was measured.</b> <b>Rule 5/4 is necessary and not sufficient, and the measurement that shows it is a null.</b> Implemented faithfully and alone (grip at lag 0) it passes <code>strikethrough</code> and moves nothing else: it leaves <b>11 of the 35 self-checking transfer cartridges failing, the same eleven that fail without it. Not one of the eleven moves.</b> That is what proves there is a second, separable question here. <b>Neither competitor&rsquo;s account says when the transfer takes the bus</b>, so there was nothing to converge on; this half is ours and it was measured. <b>Three answers, and two are refuted.</b> <b>(a) The grip is taken at the instant the transfer becomes active</b> (REFUTED: eleven of the 35 fail). <b>(b) A whole machine cycle later</b>. REFUTED <em>in both directions</em>, and this is the interesting one: it does not score slightly worse, it <b>swaps the failures</b>, every cartridge of one round that fails at lag 0 turning green while every cartridge of the other round turns red, one for one. <b>A shift that trades one set for the other is evidence the quantity is real and smaller than a machine cycle</b>: a whole cycle overshoots by as much as zero undershoots. <b>(c) A dot-resolved lag of 1 or 2 dots</b>. Satisfies both scorers at once: <code>strikethrough</code> at 0 differing pixels, pixel-exact with no tolerance, and 35 of 35 on the corpus. That sentence (the invariant) is what the measurement establishes. Read from our source: the history is recorded once per dot at the end of the dot, and the scan reads the entry from two dots back.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why 2 is a representative, not a finding.</b> Section 4 states the start-up (Rule 4/6) and its own limits, and without it there is no grid for this lag to sit on. <b>2 is a legal representative of the bound, not a finding</b>: state it outright so nobody hunts for a derivation, and <b>do not tighten it to 2 without a measurement that separates them</b>. A number promoted because it happens to be the one in the file is the silent upgrade the label rules exist to prevent. <b>The hardware session does not tighten it:</b> both values render all 35 cartridges identically. <b>What would separate 1 from 2:</b> a stimulus at dot granularity around the slot boundary (the existing cartridges step the transfer&rsquo;s start in machine cycles, so the corpus cannot address a sub-cycle offset) or a cartridge whose scan slot straddles the grip with different Y values either side. The transfer&rsquo;s own source reads are never blocked (Rule 4/12).</div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">The cursor clause is not load-bearing, and that is recorded so nobody writes it down as necessary</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-6"><b>Rule 5/6.</b> While a transfer is active, a mode-3 object fetch takes its tile number and attribute from the transfer rather than from the object. <b>Which byte of the transfer is beneath our resolution on everything we hold</b>, so a build may choose; what the shipped core does, so a build can match it where nothing can see the difference (not a claim about the hardware), is this. The fetch takes its two bytes once, on the dot the object fetch starts. Let c be the number of bytes the transfer has copied so far (0 to 159). The tile number is sprite-memory byte c &minus; 1 (byte 0 when c is 0), the byte the transfer last wrote; the attribute is byte c, the next one, still holding its old contents. The tile number goes into the even latch and the attribute into the odd latch, so they are also what the following scan or fetch sees as the latches&rsquo; held values: a following scan under a grip tests the tile number as its Y and files the attribute as its X.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> An object fetch starts on a dot after the transfer has copied 40 bytes (c = 40, bytes 0 to 39). Its tile number is sprite-memory byte 39 (the new value just copied); its attribute is byte 40 (still the old value). An object fetch that starts after the transfer becomes active and before it copies byte 0 has c = 0: tile number and attribute are both byte 0, still holding its old contents.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why it is not load-bearing.</b> Five structural readings of &ldquo;the transfer&rsquo;s cursor&rdquo; were built, and so was switching the clause off entirely: <b>all six produce a byte-identical framebuffer</b>, on all 264 board rows and all 35 cartridges. The reason is not subtle: the stacked objects are opaque under every tile the cursor can supply, so the fetch&rsquo;s choice of tile is painted over. <b>The scan-latch half alone buys the row.</b> Under the labelling rule a value that cannot be measured is not ours to state, so <b>the invariant is stated and the value is not</b>. <b>Falsifier:</b> one moved byte between two cursor readings. Read from our source (<code>OBJ_FETCH_DMA</code> 1, <code>OBJ_DMA_CURSOR</code> 1, one of the five readings above).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-7"><b>Rule 5/7.</b> <b>With no transfer active</b>, the tile number and attribute of an object fetch are the ones the scan took from the object&rsquo;s own sprite-memory entry on the dot the scan selected it (mode 2): bytes 2 and 3 of the entry at the slot being scanned, taken straight from sprite memory and past the latches, which carry only Y and X. A grip does not stop this (Rule 5/4&rsquo;s &ldquo;no read&rdquo; is the read into the latches). If a transfer is active when the fetch starts, Rule 5/6 replaces them; otherwise the fetch does not read them again, and only copies them into the latches when it starts (tile number even, attribute odd, as in Rule 5/6). The two plane bytes are read from video memory on the fetch&rsquo;s last dot, with each plane&rsquo;s address formed then through its own view of LCDC (E.1.3 and E.9&rsquo;s tags).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> The processor cannot write sprite memory during modes 2 and 3, so only a transfer can make the scan&rsquo;s copy and the entry differ, and that case is Rule 5/6. And the processor side, for completeness: a processor read on the transfer&rsquo;s bus is Rule 4/9. Read from our source.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Where this leaves the row: WON, at 0 differing pixels.</b> Both halves (Rules 5/4 and 5/5) are in this core: <code>strikethrough</code> at 0 differing pixels and the object-scan corpus at 35 of 35, with the mealybug boards, both acid tests, the 252-probe mode-3 timing table, the Mooneye and Blargg sets and the audio score unmoved by them.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>And the methodological residue.</b> A cartridge whose transfer source is byte-identical to sprite memory cannot see what the transfer does: every rule about <em>when</em> the scan reads relative to the transfer&rsquo;s write makes the same prediction on it. Station 06 is such a cartridge: when its cartridge is read rather than its output, its transfer source is byte-identical to sprite memory by construction, and it places objects 10 to 39 off-screen, so it is structurally blind to any survivor outside 0 to 9. <b>Its empty band is not a null</b> for a family of rules it has no power over. A cartridge whose proudest property is that the transfer never changes a byte is a cartridge that cannot see the thing the transfer does.</div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">Transfers and buses: ask <em>which</em> bus, not <em>whether</em></h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-8"><b>Rule 5/8.</b> The transfer does not use the processor&rsquo;s address decoder at all, so the boot program&rsquo;s mapping does not reach it: a source over <code>0000</code>&ndash;<code>00FF</code> (on the Color also <code>0200</code>&ndash;<code>08FF</code>) reads the cartridge while the boot program is mapped. The value written to <code>0xFF46</code> decides which bus the transfer occupies:' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Value written to <code>0xFF46</code> (the source&rsquo;s high byte)</th><th>Bus the transfer occupies</th><th>Chip select it asserts</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td><code>0x00&ndash;0x7F</code></td><td>External</td><td>Cartridge program memory</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>0x80&ndash;0x9F</code></td><td><b>Video RAM</b></td><td>Video RAM</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>0xA0&ndash;0xFF</code></td><td>External</td><td>Cartridge RAM</td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table>' +
      'So the question to ask is <b>which bus the transfer occupies</b>, never <em>whether</em> a transfer is running. <b>A transfer sourced from video memory does not lock the processor out of the cartridge.</b> Only sprite memory is owned (Rule 4/11); everything else stays reachable, with the conflict of Rule 5/9. This table gives the bus and select on the original; it does not classify a conflict. A conflict is decided by Rule 5/9&rsquo;s groups, where source values <code>0xA0&ndash;0xBF</code> are external, <code>0xC0&ndash;0xFD</code> work RAM and its echo, and <code>0xFE</code> and <code>0xFF</code> group <em>none</em>. On the original the two give the same conflicts, because its cartridge and work RAM share one bus; on the Color they differ and Rule 5/9 decides.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> GB-CTR is the document to reach for on disputed bus behaviour. Its chapter 8 has a section headed exactly <b>&ldquo;OAM DMA bus conflicts&rdquo;</b>. <b>The body of that section is the single word <code>TODO</code>.</b> Checked at the locus, in the published PDF. So on the one question everybody asks, the authority does not answer. What the same chapter does give is the address decoding (the table above), and that turns out to be the useful part. Measured: with the whole-bus rule (everything but high RAM blocked during a transfer), all nine of the instruction-timing cartridges that execute from cartridge memory while sourcing the transfer from video memory <em>hang</em>. The hang is the measurement, and it is the reason section 4 states the rule the way it does. Checked against two independent documents rather than argued from one: the table above, and Pan Docs, which describes the Color as putting the cartridge and work RAM on separate buses and says outright that there the processor may reach cartridge memory or cartridge RAM during a transfer sourced from work RAM. <b>And one line of Pan Docs should not be read as a description of the bus:</b> <i>&ldquo;On DMG, during OAM DMA, the CPU can access only HRAM&rdquo;</i> is the whole-bus rule, and it is guidance for the usual case (the nine cartridges above run on real hardware and would not if it were literally true). The two sentences reconcile the moment you stop asking <em>whether</em>.</div>' +
    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">A block is not a conflict, and the difference is a crash handler</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-9"><b>Rule 5/9.</b> A <b>block</b> substitutes a constant for one region (sprite memory, Rule 4/11). A <b>conflict</b> returns whatever the transfer is putting on the bus that cycle. The conflict rule classifies <em>both</em> addresses. Sort the transfer&rsquo;s source address and the processor&rsquo;s address into four groups: external (cartridge program memory and cartridge RAM), video, work RAM and its echo, and <em>none</em> (high RAM, the registers, sprite memory, all always reachable as far as this rule goes; sprite memory&rsquo;s own lock is Rule 4/11). Same group, and the processor&rsquo;s read returns the byte the transfer is moving this cycle (Rule 4/9 says which) while the processor&rsquo;s write does not land. Different group, and nothing happens: the access behaves normally. <b>The one device difference is that the original Game Boy puts the cartridge and work RAM on one bus and the Color splits them</b>, so on the original a non-video source conflicts with either of them. A source of <code>0xFE</code> or <code>0xFF</code> is in group <em>none</em>: on the original it is a non-video source like any other, and on the Color nothing conflicts with it. The conflict lasts from W+2 to W+161 (Rule 4/9).' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Transfer source (group)</th><th>Processor address groups that conflict, original Game Boy</th><th>Processor address groups that conflict, Color</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td>External (cartridge program memory or cartridge RAM)</td><td>external, work RAM and its echo</td><td>external</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Video</td><td>video</td><td>video</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Work RAM and its echo</td><td>external, work RAM and its echo</td><td>work RAM and its echo</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Any source</td><td colspan="2">group <em>none</em> never conflicts</td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> ashiepaws&rsquo; <code>bully</code> pushes a register pair to a work-RAM stack during a transfer sourced from cartridge memory and pops it back. On the original (external and work RAM on one bus) the pops read the transfer&rsquo;s bytes and the pushes do not land. On the Color (separate buses) it reads back what it pushed. A transfer sourced from video memory with the processor running from cartridge memory: different groups on both devices, so the processor runs normally.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Implement only the block and feed the processor <code>0xFF</code>, and ashiepaws&rsquo; <code>bully</code> executes <code>0xFF</code>, which is <code>RST $38</code>, and prints its own crash handler. The screen names the defect outright: <i>&ldquo;DMA bus conflict &hellip; always reads $FF&rdquo;</i>. That split is pinned by the cartridge rather than chosen: on the original it must read the transfer&rsquo;s bytes and the push must not land; on the Color it must read back what it pushed. One cartridge asserting both, and both of its rows pass. <b>DERIVED from two documents, discriminated by one cartridge.</b></div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">And one from the cartridge side: the clock latch</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-10"><b>Rule 5/10.</b> On MBC3&rsquo;s clock, <b>a processor write to a clock register reaches the counter only; a read returns the latched copy.</b></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-11"><b>Rule 5/11.</b> <b>A single write latches</b>: every write to <code>0x6000&ndash;0x7FFF</code>, of any value, copies the running counter into the readable registers. A 0-then-1 sequence is not required.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> After an opening latch, a program writes <code>0x5A</code> to <code>0x6000</code>, waits, and reads the seconds register: it reads the counter&rsquo;s seconds as of that write. It then writes <code>0xC3</code> to <code>0x6000</code> a second later and reads again: the read-back has changed by one second. Under the 0-then-1 rule neither write would latch and both reads would return the same value.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> This refutes the rule every reference gives for MBC3&rsquo;s clock: that writing 0 and then 1 to <code>0x6000&ndash;0x7FFF</code> copies the running counter into the readable registers. Not from the bench: <code>cpp/latch-rtc-test</code> declares its answer as a hex dump rather than a picture (640 nibbles of data, not something to be judged by eye) and it writes five random bytes, reads five back and writes <b>one</b> random byte to the latch region, 52 times over. After the opening pair, no <code>0x00</code> and no <code>0x01</code> is ever written there again. Two things follow, and neither is chosen. Write both the counter and the latched copy together and the read-back can never lag; the cartridge&rsquo;s declared answer needs it to lag. And under the 0&nbsp;then&nbsp;1 rule the cartridge would latch once at initialisation and print the same group 52 times, where the reference prints a different one every time. <b>CARTRIDGE-DECLARED</b>, from a reference that is a table of values rather than an image, which is as good as this kind of evidence gets short of silicon. Settled from our source: <code>RTC_LATCH_ANY_WRITE</code> 1.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>The invariant, if you build your own:</b> after the first latch, write a <em>single</em> arbitrary byte to the latch region and <b>the read-back must change</b>. If your implementation requires a 0-then-1 sequence, that write will change nothing and you will print the same group every time. That is the whole discriminator.</div>' +


    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">What the divider holds when the boot program hands off</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-12"><b>Rule 5/12.</b> The boot program runs and then jumps to the cartridge at <code>0x0100</code>. The free-running divider has been counting the whole time, so it must not be 0 at the hand-off. A core that skips the boot program and jumps straight to <code>0x0100</code> must <em>synthesise</em> the value the boot program would have left, held at the start of the machine cycle that fetches <code>0100</code> (section 3):' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Machine</th><th>Internal 16-bit counter at handoff (hex)</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td>Original Game Boy</td><td><b><code>0xABC8</code></b>, so the visible register (<code>0xFF04</code>, the top byte) reads <code>0xAB</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Game Boy Color, the revision this core targets</td><td><code>0x2674</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Game Boy Color, earliest revision</td><td><code>0x2880</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Advance, in compatibility mode</td><td><code>0x2678</code></td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table>' +
      '<b>If you model only one machine, <code>0xABC8</code> is the number.</b> This core also has a setting that ships <code>0xABCC</code> on every device and ignores the family: a coarser answer that costs the row.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> <b>A core that starts it at zero is wrong from the first instruction, and every timer-dependent title inherits the error.</b> This matters twice over: once for cores that supply a boot program (Rule 5/13), and once for cores that skip it.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>How it was established, and what that does and does not prove.</b> Not on a real console. Each value was <b>solved from six declared assert values inside a self-checking test cartridge</b>: one cartridge for the original machine, one for the Color family. Those cartridges assert a <em>phase</em>, six register values in sequence, not a single number, so passing pins the divider to the machine cycle across the whole boot program rather than at one instant. <b>The Color family&rsquo;s two cartridges assert different phases and a machine can only pass one of them</b>, which is what makes the family split a measurement rather than a preference. Independently confirmed by running the real boot program rather than synthesising the handoff: the register reads <code>AB</code> exactly as expected, and the cycle count at handoff is identical to an independent trace (23,440,324 T-cycles, 2,205,105 steps). <b>CARTRIDGE-DECLARED</b>, corroborated by a cycle-exact boot run. <b>Two limitations, stated:</b> the original-machine and Color values live in one setting in our source and cannot be turned off independently, so no experiment we have run separates &ldquo;the DMG value is right&rdquo; from &ldquo;the CGB value is right&rdquo;; and neither was read off a real console.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-5-13"><b>Rule 5/13.</b> With the real boot program, the divider must lead &ldquo;T-cycles since the first fetch&rdquo; by one machine cycle: the 16-bit internal counter is set to <b>4</b> at power-on, which is the start of the machine cycle that fetches the boot program&rsquo;s first opcode (address <code>0000</code>), before that cycle&rsquo;s four timer ticks, so the counter stands at 8 when that fetch reads. It is the same placement as the no-boot divider value of section 3 (held at the start of the cycle that fetches <code>0100</code>).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> Power-on: counter = 4. The first machine cycle&rsquo;s four timer ticks take it to 8, and then the opcode at <code>0000</code> is read. Every later opcode fetch advances the timer by four T-cycles before its read in the same way. Started at 0 instead, the counter would reach the cartridge one machine cycle (4 T-cycles) behind.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why: a one-machine-cycle residue nobody has closed.</b> Running the real boot program lands the internal counter at <code>0xABC4</code>, while the no-boot shortcut resets it to <code>0xABC8</code>, and <b><code>0xABC8</code> is the value that passes the cartridge</b>. That is a gap of 4 T-cycles, one machine cycle, <em>with the cycle count at handoff already exact</em>. <b>So the divider is not simply &ldquo;T-cycles since the first fetch&rdquo;. It leads by one machine cycle</b>, and this core carries that as a constant (Rule 5/13). MECHANISM for the placement, read from our source (every opcode fetch advances the timer by four before its read; <code>BOOT_DIV_START</code>). <b>That 4 is stated outright and it is a reconciliation of two measurements rather than a derivation. It is not explained.</b> A reader who finds the explanation has found something this page does not have.</div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">Four stations that produced no finding, written up anyway</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why they are here.</b> Because a reader deserves to know which questions are open and which captures are sitting unexamined, and because a built cartridge is not a finding. The four are stations <b>02, 03, 07 and 08</b>, written up in three bullets below because one bullet covers two of them.' +
      '<ul class="gbemu-bullets">' +
        '<li><b>Station 02: the noise shift register, widened mid-run. CAPTURED, NEVER ANALYSED.</b> Three recordings exist and <em>nothing in the record reads them</em>: no bench sheet, no result note, no entry anywhere. What our core implements is appendix B.6: <b>there is no 7-bit register; there is one fifteen-stage register, always</b>, and the width bit selects only where the feedback bit is deposited, so <b>stages 7 to 14 keep shifting in narrow mode: not idle, not held, not cleared</b>, and the change is audible with no retrigger. One timing constant goes with it and is <b>CARTRIDGE-DECLARED, 128 of 128 and its mirror</b>: the write to the noise frequency/width register <b>lands at the end of its machine cycle, not the start</b>. Applied on entry, our stream diverges in 62 of 128 declared bytes; deferred to the end it is exact in all 128. <b>The invariant the recordings would test, which needs no source code:</b> after eight narrow steps, <code>b[j+8] == b[j]</code> for <i>j</i> = 0 to 6 (the high half of the register mirrors the low half). <b>That check has not been run.</b> And the honest state of the disagreement: against an independent implementation the control and the 15&rarr;7 case agree at 1.0000 and <b>the 7&rarr;15 case reads 0.5828</b>, a real live disagreement in which that implementation&rsquo;s behaviour is a <em>third</em> thing neither model reproduces. <b>Nothing in that comparison is hardware-referenced.</b></li>' +
        '<li><b>Station 03: does the sweep write back after an overflow? NOT MEASURED.</b> Nothing was captured; there is no result note, no bench sheet and no prediction script. The two rules are entirely different machines rather than a rounding difference: <b>an overflow disables the channel and writes back nothing</b>, so the shadow freezes and every later step recomputes the same overflow (what we do), against <b>the sweep keeps clocking and keeps writing back the truncated result</b> (the rival). <b>Nothing in the two large processor suites reaches this case and both pass for us</b>, which is why a bench station was designed. Two predicted read-back values circulate in our record and <b>they are software, not silicon</b>: they were predicted under the rival rule and then measured on <em>another implementation</em>, where both came out exact with no free parameter. <b>No console has been asked.</b> Three cartridges for this station exist, referenced by nothing. <b>Nothing may be built on them.</b></li>' +
        '<li><b>Stations 07 and 08: built, never run.</b> Both are Game Boy Color cartridges (they read <code>PCM12</code>/<code>PCM34</code>, which do not exist on the original), both write self-grading save files with a control block that prints <code>VOID</code> if the controls disagree with already-known answers, and <b>both capture folders are empty</b>. They are listed so nobody mistakes a built cartridge for a finding. <b>Nothing on this page rests on either.</b></li>' +
      '</ul></div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">What the reading bought, with its denominator</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>What Rules 5/1 to 5/3 are worth.</b> With them, these four of blargg&rsquo;s seven <code>oam_bug</code> cartridges pass: <code>4-scanline_timing</code>, <code>5-timing_bug</code>, <code>6-timing_no_bug</code> and <code>8-instr_effect</code> (the last one only with the start-of-cycle sampling of Rule 5/2). <b>They cost nothing elsewhere:</b> all 24 mealybug cartridges are at 0 differing pixels, both dmg-acid2 runs are pixel-perfect, and a counter on the three corruption sites returned 0 over all 252 mode-3 probes with the positive control reading 136 first, so the null is the hardware&rsquo;s and not a dead instrument.</div>' +
    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">One clock, sampled at two instants, and the diagnosis that was wrong</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why Rule 5/2 samples every event at the start of its machine cycle.</b> The two families of hook read <b>the same clock at two different instants of the same machine cycle</b>. The increment hooks fire from the opcode before that cycle&rsquo;s tick, so they read the dot counter at the cycle&rsquo;s <em>start</em>; the read and write hooks live inside the bus, which ticks first, so they read it at the <em>end</em>. A machine cycle is four dots and the row rule divides by four, so without the correction of Rule 5/2 step 2 <b>the bus side is exactly one row high</b>: always, invisibly, and only where the two families meet. Attribute every event to the start of its machine cycle and no bus address moves, no tick moves, and instruction, memory and interrupt timing are bit-identical. <b>MECHANISM</b>: two sample points on one clock reconciled, not a constant swept.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>The diagnosis that is wrong.</b> The tempting diagnosis for an <code>8-instr_effect</code> failure at its third sub-test is that <code>pop</code> and <code>push</code> assert their bus addresses one machine cycle after the cycle that carries the increment: a core-wide processor bus-placement question with a frightening blast radius, and a good reason to leave it alone. <b>That diagnosis is wrong. Do not take it on trust; re-derive it, and the row closes.</b></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example: the rows <code>8-instr_effect</code>&rsquo;s sub-tests corrupt.</b> With Rule 5/2, <code>pop bc</code> hits rows 4&ndash;7, <code>push bc</code> rows 6&ndash;8, and <code>ld a,(hl&plusmn;)</code> rows <code>r&minus;2</code> to <code>r</code>. All six dumps reproduce the silicon byte for byte and the cartridge passes.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>One residue is named rather than claimed.</b> The start-of-cycle rule has a branch for a machine cycle that began on the previous line (Rule 5/2 step 4), and <b>that branch fired zero times across sixty test cartridges</b>. It is unmeasured, not validated, and the denominator is printed here for the same reason it is printed in the source.</div>' +
  '</section>' +


  /* ---------------------------------------------------------------- M5 */
  '<section class="sect" id="gbe-regs"><h3 class="sect-h">Appendix A. Every register, every bit</h3>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable">The sections above are about the behaviours that are hard. This appendix is the part that is merely <em>necessary</em>: the map an implementer cannot derive and must not guess. It is transcription from the decode paths of the core the rest of this page describes, so the 264-row board is what verifies it: a register decoded wrongly here does not produce a subtle timing error, it produces a machine that never boots. In the bit tables, bit 7 is the most significant bit and bit 0 the least.</p>' +
    '<p class="gbemu-key"><b>What is a requirement and what is ours.</b> Only two things about a register are observable: <b>the byte the processor reads back</b>, and <b>the effect of the byte it writes</b>. Everything else, whether you keep a 16-bit divider counter or a T-cycle accumulator, whether the picture registers live in a struct or in a 128-byte array, whether you store STAT with its read-only bits or compose them at the read: is yours to choose and nothing on the board can tell. Where a row below describes a <em>structure</em> rather than an observable, it says so.</p>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">A.1: Pad, serial, timer, interrupt latch</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-1-1"><b>Rule A.1/1.</b> These registers must read and write as this table says:' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Name</th><th>Bits (bit 7 most significant)</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF00</code></td><td>P1 / JOYP</td><td><b>7&ndash;6</b> unused, always read 1. <b>5</b> select the action group, <b>write only</b>, <em>active low</em>. <b>4</b> select the direction group, write only, active low. <b>3&ndash;0</b> the selected inputs, <b>read only, active low</b>: bit 3 Start/Down, bit 2 Select/Up, bit 1 B/Left, bit 0 A/Right. Only bits 5&ndash;4 are storage; a write to any other bit is discarded</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF01</code></td><td>SB</td><td>The shift byte. Eight bits, fully read/write, no mask</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF02</code></td><td>SC</td><td><b>7</b> transfer in progress: set to start, cleared by the machine when the eighth bit has moved; a write with bit 7 clear stores it while a transfer in flight runs on to its eighth bit (Rule A.1/6). <b>1</b> fast clock (Colour only; inert here). <b>0</b> clock source, 1 = internal. Reads back ORed with <code>0x7E</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF04</code></td><td>DIV</td><td>Read: <b>the top eight bits of the 16-bit counter of section 4</b>. Write: <b>any</b> value zeroes <em>the whole counter</em>, not just the visible byte (Rule 4/1)</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF05</code></td><td>TIMA</td><td>The counting register. Eight bits, no mask. See section 4 for the <b>three</b> writes that do not behave, and for which of the two overflow windows each one lands in (Rule 4/4)</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF06</code></td><td>TMA</td><td>The value loaded into TIMA on overflow. Eight bits, no mask</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF07</code></td><td>TAC</td><td><b>7&ndash;3</b> unused, read 1. <b>2</b> enable. <b>1&ndash;0</b> clock select, and what it selects is <em>which bit of the 16-bit counter is watched</em>: <code>00</code>&rarr;bit&nbsp;9, <code>01</code>&rarr;bit&nbsp;3, <code>10</code>&rarr;bit&nbsp;5, <code>11</code>&rarr;bit&nbsp;7. Reads back ORed with <code>0xF8</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF0F</code></td><td>IF</td><td><b>7&ndash;5</b> unused, <b>read 1</b>: the byte is ORed with <code>0xE0</code> on the way out. <b>4</b> pad, <b>3</b> serial, <b>2</b> timer, <b>1</b> picture status, <b>0</b> vertical blank. A write keeps only bits 4&ndash;0</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FFFF</code></td><td>IE</td><td>Same bit assignment as IF, and <b>all eight bits are plain storage</b>: no mask on either side. The upper three are unused by the dispatcher and read back exactly as written, which is the whole of what Mooneye <code>if_ie_registers</code> checks</td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-1-2"><b>Rule A.1/2.</b> <b>The pad&rsquo;s two groups are ANDed, not multiplexed.</b> Each of bits 3&ndash;0 reads 0 if a held input of a selected group is on that bit, and 1 otherwise. With both select bits low, a held action button masks a direction on the same bit; with both high the low nibble reads <code>0xF</code>.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> A is held and Right is not. P1 written <code>0x10</code> (bit 5 low: action group selected): bit 0 reads 0. P1 written <code>0x20</code> (direction group only): bit 0 reads 1. P1 written <code>0x00</code> (both groups): bit 0 reads 0, although Right is not held. P1 written <code>0x30</code>: the low nibble reads <code>0xF</code>.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> That is why the power-on value of this register matters and why section 3 spends a paragraph on it. <b>DERIVED</b>, and the AND is what this core implements.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-1-3"><b>Rule A.1/3.</b> <b>A pad interrupt is raised on a newly pressed input that is in a currently selected group</b>, not on release, and not on a press in a deselected group. With a Super Game Boy attached only player 1&rsquo;s buttons raise it (Rule A.6/7). <b>When, exactly</b>: at the moment the host changes the buttons (between machine cycles; the host sets them once per frame or per input event), using the selection in <code>P1</code> as it stands then. A later <code>P1</code> write that selects a group whose button is already held raises nothing: only a new press does. While the machine is stopped by <code>STOP</code> (D.9), it tests for a selected line reading low at every machine cycle, so the stop ends at the first machine cycle after the press.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> P1 selects the direction group only. The player presses A: no interrupt (the action group is not selected). The program then writes P1 to select the action group while A is still held: no interrupt (no new press). The player releases A and presses it again: IF bit 4 is set at that press.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Read from our source.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-1-4"><b>Rule A.1/4.</b> <b>Serial, as this core does it.</b> The internal clock is divided from the same free-running counter as the timer (bit 8), <b>tapped one machine cycle ahead</b>: the shift clock is bit 8 of the counter plus 4 T-cycles, not of the counter itself. Each falling edge (1 to 0) of that shift clock moves one bit, while a transfer is in progress. Its edges align to a divider reset and not to the SC write. The edge detector samples the shift clock on every T-cycle, with or without a transfer in progress.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> The counter goes from <code>0x01FB</code> to <code>0x01FC</code>: counter + 4 goes from <code>0x01FF</code> to <code>0x0200</code>, so the tapped bit 8 falls from 1 to 0 and one bit shifts. The counter itself reaches <code>0x0200</code> four T-cycles later, which is why the tap is one machine cycle ahead. The next falling edge is 512 T-cycles later.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> That lead is what lets <code>boot_sclk_align-dmgABCmgb</code> and <code>boot_div-dmgABCmgb</code> pass together; it is measured, and which of three places the one machine cycle really belongs in (the divider&rsquo;s phase, the serial edge detector, or the processor&rsquo;s interrupt sample) is NOT ESTABLISHED. Settled from our source: <code>SERIAL_TAP_LEAD</code> 4.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-1-5"><b>Rule A.1/5.</b> <b>A divider write can itself shift a bit</b>: the counter is zeroed, the detector keeps its last level, and if the tapped bit was high, the first T-cycle tick after the write sees it low and shifts one bit, as any falling edge does (the frame sequencer sees the same kind of fall, section 15).</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Read from our source.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-1-6"><b>Rule A.1/6.</b> Eight falling edges move eight bits; with no partner attached, ones shift in, so an unconnected machine reads back <code>0xFF</code> and the serial interrupt fires on the eighth. With the <em>external</em> clock selected and no partner, the transfer never completes, <b>and that is what this core does</b>: bit 7 stays set, SB does not shift and no serial interrupt is raised, for as long as no partner clocks it (the clock comes from the partner, and there is none). An SC write during a transfer: with bits 7 and 0 both set, the count of eight restarts, with SB and the shift clock untouched. Any other value only stores its bits: the transfer runs on, and the eighth bit still clears bit 7 and raises the serial interrupt, so a write with bit 7 clear does not stop it.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Clearing bit 7 at once instead is wrong: two licensed titles that look for a link partner at start-up read that as a partner answering, and wait for it for ever. Blargg&rsquo;s suites are not affected: they start their transfers on the <em>internal</em> clock (<code>0x81</code>), which the rule above times.</div>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable"><b>Elsewhere:</b> the Game Boy Printer, the extra hardware around the console and A.1.1 (the Super Game Boy shelf) are in the area &ldquo;Super Game Boy and link devices&rdquo;, file <code>gb-sgb-emulation-tab.js</code> (<a href="#gbe-sgb">here</a>).</p>' +
    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">A.2: The five interrupts, their vectors, and their order</h4>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable">Section 2 says &ldquo;load the vector&rdquo; and section 14 discusses the status line at length; neither says <em>which</em> vector, and the vertical-blank interrupt appears nowhere else on this page at all. Here they are.</p>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-2-1"><b>Rule A.2/1.</b> The five interrupts are:' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>IF / IE bit</th><th>Source</th><th>Vector (address)</th><th>What raises it</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td><b>0</b> (highest priority)</td><td>Vertical blank</td><td><code>0x0040</code></td><td>The picture processor entering mode 1, at the start of line 144</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><b>1</b></td><td>Picture status (STAT / LCD)</td><td><code>0x0048</code></td><td>The <em>rising edge</em> of the single ORed status line: Rule 4/14, A.3 and section 14</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><b>2</b></td><td>Timer</td><td><code>0x0050</code></td><td>TIMA overflowing. The request <em>is</em> the carry, so it is set at the overflow cycle and not at the reload: section 4 (Rule 4/3)</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><b>3</b></td><td>Serial</td><td><code>0x0058</code></td><td>The eighth bit of a transfer having moved</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><b>4</b> (lowest)</td><td>Pad</td><td><code>0x0060</code></td><td>A newly pressed input in a selected group (Rule A.1/3)</td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table>' +
      '<b>Vector = <code>0x40 + bit &times; 8</code>, and priority is simply the lowest set bit of <code>IE &amp; IF</code>.</b> There is no separate priority table to get wrong.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> <b>MECHANISM</b>: one expression produces both columns, and writing it that way is what makes the two impossible to disagree.</div>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable">Three details of the dispatch that the five-cycle breakdown in section 2 does not carry, each of which is a test (Rules A.2/2 to A.2/4):</p>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-2-2"><b>Rule A.2/2.</b> <b>The master enable is cleared first</b>, before any of the five cycles runs.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-2-3"><b>Rule A.2/3.</b> <b><code>IE &amp; IF</code> is re-sampled after the high byte of the program counter has been pushed</b>, not before the dispatch begins, and before the low byte is pushed (D.12.2 places the sample <em>between</em> the two pushes). A push that lands on <code>0xFFFF</code> before the sample therefore overwrites IE mid-dispatch, and <b>the stack pointer that can <em>redirect</em> this dispatch is <code>0x0000</code> and only <code>0x0000</code></b>. Two stack pointers put a push byte on <code>0xFFFF</code> (<code>0x0000</code> through the high byte at <code>SP&minus;1</code>, <code>0x0001</code> through the low byte at <code>SP&minus;2</code>), but with <code>SP&nbsp;=&nbsp;0x0001</code> the write to IE lands <b>after</b> the vector has already been decided: it changes IE for the next dispatch and cannot touch this one.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-2-4"><b>Rule A.2/4.</b> <b>If that re-sample leaves no bit set, the machine jumps to <code>0x0000</code></b> and clears no request bit.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> IE = <code>0x01</code>, IF = <code>0x01</code> (vertical blank), SP = <code>0x0000</code>, PC = <code>0x1234</code>. The master enable is cleared; the high byte <code>0x12</code> is pushed to <code>0xFFFF</code>, so IE becomes <code>0x12</code>; the re-sample reads <code>IE &amp; IF</code> = <code>0x12 &amp; 0x01</code> = 0, so the machine jumps to <code>0x0000</code> and IF stays <code>0x01</code>. With SP = <code>0x0001</code> instead, the high byte goes to <code>0x0000</code>, the re-sample reads <code>0x01 &amp; 0x01</code> and picks vector <code>0x0040</code>, and only then does the low byte <code>0x34</code> land in IE.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> <b>DERIVED</b> from the push arithmetic and the sample point, and pinned by Mooneye <code>ie_push</code>. <b>Falsifier:</b> a redirect observed with <code>SP&nbsp;=&nbsp;0x0001</code>, which would move the sample to after M4. The jump to <code>0x0000</code> is not an error case: it is the same test as above, and a core that cannot produce it has the sample in the wrong place.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-2-5"><b>Rule A.2/5.</b> The request bit of the chosen source is cleared as part of the dispatch, and <b>only that one</b>.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-2-6"><b>Rule A.2/6.</b> HALT wakes on <code>IE &amp; IF</code> non-zero <em>irrespective</em> of the master enable: the enable decides whether a vector is taken, not whether the machine wakes.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Section 2 has the bug that lives in the gap between those two.</div>' +


    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">A.3: The display block, <code>FF40</code>&ndash;<code>FF4B</code></h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-3-1"><b>Rule A.3/1.</b> These registers must read and write as this table says:' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Name</th><th>Bits (bit 7 most significant)</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF40</code></td><td>LCDC</td><td><b>7</b> picture processor on. <b>6</b> window map select: 0&rarr;<code>0x9800</code>, 1&rarr;<code>0x9C00</code>. <b>5</b> window enable. <b>4</b> tile-data select: 1&rarr;unsigned from <code>0x8000</code>, 0&rarr;signed from <code>0x9000</code> (appendix C). <b>3</b> background map select, same two addresses. <b>2</b> object height: 0&rarr;8 rows, 1&rarr;16. <b>1</b> objects enabled. <b>0</b> background and window enabled: clear it and the background pixel becomes colour 0 before the palette. <b>All eight bits are read/write with no mask</b>, and every one of them is read <em>live, mid-line</em> by the sections above</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF41</code></td><td>STAT</td><td><b>7</b> unused, <b>always reads 1</b>. <b>6</b> enable the coincidence source. <b>5</b> enable the mode-2 source. <b>4</b> enable the mode-1 source. <b>3</b> enable the mode-0 source. <b>2</b> coincidence flag, <b>read only</b>: LY equals LYC, <b>masked by the comparator&rsquo;s blind window</b>. <b>The window is not optional</b>: it opens every ordinary line (section 14). <b>At the no-boot hand-over it is not armed</b>: the hand-over state stores the flag clear and the blind window at 0, so the comparator sets the flag as dot 0 is processed (LY and LYC are both <code>0</code>), and the first value any instruction can read is <code>0x86</code>, as section 3 says (INSTRUMENT-MEASURED). The blind window first runs at the next line boundary. <b>1&ndash;0</b> current mode (the reported mode), <b>read only</b>. <b>A write keeps only bits 6&ndash;3</b> (mask <code>0x78</code>)</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF42</code></td><td>SCY</td><td>Background vertical scroll. Eight bits, wraps</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF43</code></td><td>SCX</td><td>Background horizontal scroll. <b>Two consumers, not one</b>: the high five bits pick the map cell, the low three are the line&rsquo;s fine-scroll discard. Section 13 is about them not settling together</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF44</code></td><td>LY</td><td>The line being drawn, 0&ndash;153. <b>Read only</b>: a write is discarded entirely, it does not reset the counter. <b>Reads <code>0</code> for as long as the display is off</b>: switching it off clears the line counter, and the value the processor reads is a view of that counter, not a second one, so a switch-off taken in vertical blank must not leave 144&ndash;153 readable. A game that waits for <code>LY = 0</code> with the display off hangs forever otherwise</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF45</code></td><td>LYC</td><td>The line compared against LY. Eight bits, no mask. A write re-evaluates the comparator and the status line at the moment it lands, which is how a write can raise the status line mid-line. A write that lands inside a blind window (Rule 14/14) cannot raise the flag: the mask of Rule 14/15 applies to this re-evaluation too. When it lands, in dots, is E.1.1&rsquo;s LYC row: on the original after dot N + 4 has been processed (LYC&rsquo;s lead of &minus;1), so the comparison made on N + 4 still uses the old LYC and the new one is first seen from N + 5. &ldquo;At the moment it lands&rdquo; and &ldquo;from N + 5&rdquo; are the same instant on two scales</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF46</code></td><td>DMA</td><td>Write: the source&rsquo;s high byte: the transfer copies 160 bytes from <code>value&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;0x100</code> to <code>0xFE00</code> (Rule 4/6). <b>Readable</b>, returning the last value written</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF47</code></td><td>BGP</td><td>Four 2-bit shades: <b>bits 1&ndash;0 for colour 0</b>, 3&ndash;2 for colour 1, 5&ndash;4 for colour 2, 7&ndash;6 for colour 3</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF48</code></td><td>OBP0</td><td>Same layout. <b>Bits 1&ndash;0 are never consulted</b>: an object&rsquo;s colour 0 is transparency and is not looked up</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF49</code></td><td>OBP1</td><td>Same</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF4A</code></td><td>WY</td><td>The line on which the window becomes eligible. Section 11: this is a latch condition, not a comparison repeated per pixel</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF4B</code></td><td>WX</td><td>The window&rsquo;s left edge <b>plus 7</b>. <code>WX = 7</code> is column 0; values below 7 are the case section 11 spends a subsection on</td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-3-2"><b>Rule A.3/2.</b> <b>STAT is one signal, not four interrupts</b> (Rule 4/14). The four sources and their conditions:' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Enabled by (STAT bit)</th><th>Condition</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td>Coincidence</td><td>bit 6</td><td>The comparator says LY = LYC. Reported at bit 2 whether or not the source is enabled</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Mode 2</td><td>bit 5</td><td>The sprite scan, <b>and the line on which vertical blank begins</b>, where the mode is already 1. It does <em>not</em> fire on the rest of vertical blank</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Mode 1</td><td>bit 4</td><td>Vertical blank</td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td>Mode 0</td><td>bit 3</td><td>Horizontal blank</td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why (the mode-2 row).</b> A published document says the mode-2 source fires on the rest of vertical blank, and the test that counts loop iterations until the interrupt arrives says otherwise.</div>' +
    '<p class="hw-readable"><b>Two facts about STAT that a bit table cannot carry, and both are tests</b> (Rules A.3/3 and A.3/4).</p>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-3-3"><b>Rule A.3/3.</b> <b>The mode the processor reads (the reported mode) and the mode the interrupt logic sees (the interrupt-logic mode) are two different signals</b>, and a build must keep them as two.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> This core keeps them in separate variables, because holding them in one makes the line&rsquo;s phase and the interrupt&rsquo;s phase a single degree of freedom and no setting of it satisfies both.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-3-4"><b>Rule A.3/4.</b> <b>With the picture processor off the whole comparator stops, output included</b>: the status line freezes at its last value rather than dropping, so switching the processor off and on again with the coincidence still true produces no rising edge and no interrupt. A switch-off does not re-evaluate the comparator.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> Mooneye <code>stat_lyc_onoff</code> requires that, against a published document that says the line reads 0 while the processor is off. <b>The test wins.</b></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-3-5"><b>Rule A.3/5.</b> Separately, and this one <em>is</em> a read-path rule: with the processor off the mode bits read 0, while the coincidence bit keeps its value across the power-off.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-3-6"><b>Rule A.3/6.</b> <b>The comparator first evaluates after the screen is switched on at the enabling write itself.</b> On the write that sets LCDC bit 7, in this order:' +
      '<ol class="gbemu-steps">' +
        '<li>The line counter is set to 0.</li>' +
        '<li>In the same instant, before any dot of the new line is processed (E.2: the enable write&rsquo;s own cycle processes no dot), LY = 0 is compared with LYC and the status line is re-evaluated.</li>' +
        '<li>A rise there sets the request bit at the write. Because it is a coincidence-sourced rise on line 0, both dispatch holds of E.6 are armed with it, each at its own length from the write: 3 dots for the line-0 hold and 1 dot for the coincidence hold (section 14 and E.6).</li>' +
      '</ol></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> Dots here are the power-on line&rsquo;s, whose dot counter starts at 8 (E.2), on the event scale. Measured on the shipped core (the probe <code>test/tabprobe</code>), in the state <code>stat_lyc_onoff</code>&rsquo;s fourth switch-on makes (LY = LYC = 0, the coincidence flag clear and the line low before the write): the flag, the line and the request bit are all set when dot 8 is processed; the coincidence hold is spent as dot 8 is processed and the line-0 hold reads 0 from dot 10, the third dot of the first machine cycle after the write.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> <code>stat_lyc_onoff</code> turns on exactly this. Built so that the comparator first evaluates on the line&rsquo;s own dots instead, our core raises it on dot 9 and <code>stat_lyc_onoff</code> fails; nothing else among the 66 original-machine Mooneye cartridges moves. INSTRUMENT-MEASURED on our core; the placement at the write is pinned by that one cartridge and by nothing else we own.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-3-7"><b>Rule A.3/7.</b> Post-boot values for all of these are in section 3. The picture block&rsquo;s registers are not masked on write (STAT alone is), so a core that stores LCDC, SCY, SCX, LYC, BGP, OBP0, OBP1, WY and WX as plain bytes is correct.</div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">A.4: What an original Game Boy does <em>not</em> have</h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-4-1"><b>Rule A.4/1.</b> Every address in <code>FF4C</code>&ndash;<code>FF7F</code> is unmapped on this machine and <b>reads <code>0xFF</code></b>. The one exception is <code>FF50</code>: writing a value with bit 0 set unmaps the boot program, permanently.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why.</b> That is not a tidiness question. Blargg&rsquo;s combined <code>cpu_instrs</code> cartridge tests the speed register at <code>FF4D</code> with <code>ldh a,(FF4D) / rlca / ret c</code> and expects the carry to be set; a core reading 0 there falls into the Colour speed-switch path, executes a real STOP and hangs at test 03. <b>DERIVED</b> from Mooneye <code>bits/unused_hwio-GS</code>, which sweeps the whole region, and then confirmed by that failure.</div>' +

    '<h4 class="gbemu-sub">A.5: The read-back OR table for <code>FF00</code>&ndash;<code>FF7F</code></h4>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-rule" id="gbr-A-5-1"><b>Rule A.5/1.</b> Unimplemented registers, and the unused <em>bits</em> of implemented ones, read as <b>1</b> on this machine and not 0. A processor read of an address in <code>FF00</code>&ndash;<code>FF7F</code> returns the register&rsquo;s content ORed with that address&rsquo;s <b>effective mask</b>. The effective mask is the entry in the table below, except for the five addresses of A.5.1&rsquo;s table, whose effective masks are given there. Rows are sixteen addresses wide; the first entry of the <code>FF00</code> row is <code>FF00</code> itself.' +
      '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Row (first address of the sixteen)</th><th>Sixteen OR masks, hex, for row + 0 to row + F</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF00</code></td><td><code>00 00 00 FF&nbsp; 00 00 00 00&nbsp; FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF 00</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF10</code></td><td><code>80 3F 00 FF&nbsp; BF FF 3F 00&nbsp; FF BF 7F FF&nbsp; 9F FF BF FF</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF20</code></td><td><code>FF 00 00 BF&nbsp; 00 00 70 FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF30</code></td><td><code>00 00 00 00&nbsp; 00 00 00 00&nbsp; 00 00 00 00&nbsp; 00 00 00 00</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF40</code></td><td><code>00 00 00 00&nbsp; 00 00 00 00&nbsp; 00 00 00 00&nbsp; FF FF FF FF</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF50</code></td><td><code>FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF60</code></td><td><code>FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF</code></td></tr>' +
        '<tr><td><code>FF70</code></td><td><code>FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF&nbsp; FF FF FF FF</code></td></tr>' +
      '</tbody></table></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-example"><b>Example.</b> TAC (<code>FF07</code>) written <code>0x05</code> reads back <code>0xFD</code> (<code>0x05 | 0xF8</code>, A.5.1). NR10 (<code>FF10</code>) written <code>0x00</code> reads back <code>0x80</code> (the table). <code>FF4C</code> reads <code>0xFF</code> whatever was written. A wave-memory byte (<code>FF30</code>) written <code>0x5A</code> reads back <code>0x5A</code>.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Why there are two tables.</b> <b>This is the fall-through table</b>: in this core it is a 128-byte array consulted only for addresses the I/O decoder did not answer first, so five of its entries are <em>dead</em> and are marked in A.5.1. Read the effective masks in the second table with it: together they are the read-back an implementer has to reproduce.</div>' +
    '<p class="gbemu-key"><b>A.5.1: The five dead entries, and the masks that actually apply.</b> P1, SC, TAC, IF, STAT <em>and the whole audio block</em> are answered by their own decoder before the array is reached, so each applies its own mask and the array prints <code>00</code> for it. <b>A reader copying the table as the heading promises (&ldquo;the read-back OR table&rdquo;) builds five registers that read back with their unused bits clear, which is wrong on hardware and breaks Mooneye <code>bits/unused_hwio</code> and every cartridge that tests a flag by reading it back.</b> The entries are <code>00</code> because those five addresses never reach the array, not because the mask is zero:</p>' +
    '<table class="gbemu-table"><thead><tr><th>Address</th><th>Register</th><th>Array entry (hex)</th><th><b>Effective OR (hex)</b></th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
      '<tr><td><code>FF00</code></td><td>P1 / JOYP</td><td><code>00</code> (dead)</td><td><b><code>C0</code></b></td><td>Bits 7 and 6 do not exist and read 1. The decoder returns <code>0xC0 OR select OR inputs</code></td></tr>' +
      '<tr><td><code>FF02</code></td><td>SC</td><td><code>00</code> (dead)</td><td><b><code>7E</code></b></td><td>Only bit 7 (transfer) and bit 0 (clock source) exist on the original machine; bits 6&ndash;1 read 1</td></tr>' +
      '<tr><td><code>FF07</code></td><td>TAC</td><td><code>00</code> (dead)</td><td><b><code>F8</code></b></td><td>Three bits of register, five bits of nothing</td></tr>' +
      '<tr><td><code>FF0F</code></td><td>IF</td><td><code>00</code> (dead)</td><td><b><code>E0</code></b></td><td>Five request bits; the top three read 1. <b>This one is load-bearing</b>: code that reads IF, sets a bit and writes it back will write the top three as whatever it read</td></tr>' +
      '<tr><td><code>FF41</code></td><td>STAT</td><td><code>00</code> (dead)</td><td><b><code>80</code></b></td><td>Bit 7 always reads 1. The decoder builds the byte: <code>0x80 OR enables OR coincidence OR (LCD on ? reported mode : 0)</code></td></tr>' +
    '</tbody></table>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>The audio block is the opposite case.</b> <code>FF10</code>&ndash;<code>FF3F</code> is answered by the sound unit before the array is reached, so those entries are dead <em>as a code path</em>, but <b>the bytes printed in the <code>FF10</code> and <code>FF20</code> rows above are correct and are the masks you must implement</b>, which is exactly why the footer sends you to appendix B with them. <b>Read the <code>FF10</code>&ndash;<code>FF30</code> rows with appendix B open</b>: they are the sound block&rsquo;s masks, the table section 17 calls a hard invariant, and B.3 names which register each byte belongs to. The <code>FF30</code> row is zero because wave memory is storage: it reads back exactly what was written. <code>FF40</code>&ndash;<code>FF4B</code> are zero <em>in the array</em> because those registers are decoded above it; of them only STAT has bits that do not exist, and it is in the table above.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>The invariant, stated so you can check yourself without our array, and the three preconditions it holds under.</b> For every address in <code>FF00</code>&ndash;<code>FF7F</code>, write <code>0x00</code> then read: <b>the byte you read back is the OR mask for that address.</b> Unimplemented addresses return <code>FF</code>; implemented ones return their own unused bits. That is one 128-byte sweep, it needs no documentation, and it is how the rows above were checked. <b>It is only a valid sweep under three conditions:</b>' +
      '<ul class="gbemu-bullets">' +
        '<li><b>The display must be OFF</b>: clear <code>LCDC</code> bit 7 before the sweep. With it running, <code>FF41</code> bits 0&ndash;1 are the live mode and <code>FF44</code> is the live line, so both return display state rather than a mask, and both return a <em>different</em> byte on consecutive reads.</li>' +
        '<li><b>The coincidence bit must be clear.</b> <code>FF41</code> bit 2 is <b>retained across a power-off</b> (turning the display off does not clear it), so set <code>LYC</code> to a line the display did not last compare equal to, or the sweep reads <code>84</code> where the table says <code>80</code> and the table is right.</li>' +
        '<li><b>No button and no direction may be held</b>, for the reason in the next paragraph.</li>' +
      '</ul></div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>And <code>FF00</code> is excluded outright: no write makes the sweep report its mask.</b> The decoder returns <code>0xC0 | (select &amp; 0x30) | inputs</code>. Writing <code>0x00</code> writes the select bits <em>low</em>, which selects <b>both</b> pad groups, and the four input bits then read the pad. With nothing held the sweep returns <b><code>CF</code></b>, not the <code>C0</code> in the table above, which is bits 7 and 6 and is correct. Write <code>0x30</code> instead and it returns <code>FF</code>. The two select bits read back what was written and the low nibble is live, so <b>this address has no value that isolates its mask</b>, and the sweep is measuring the pad, not the read-back.</div>' +
    '<div class="gbemu-why"><b>Falsifier.</b> <b>With the display off, the coincidence bit clear, nothing held on the pad, and <code>FF00</code> excluded, any address where your sweep disagrees with the two tables here is one of them wrong, and the sweep wins.</b> Outside those conditions the tables win and the sweep is measuring something else. <b>The unconditional form of that falsifier (no preconditions and no exclusions) is the trap</b>: applied as written it corrects three correct masks into wrong ones (<code>FF00</code>&rsquo;s <code>C0</code>, <code>FF41</code>&rsquo;s <code>80</code>, and <code>FF44</code>&rsquo;s array entry) and breaks the joypad rows in the process. One further caution rather than an exclusion: <code>FF04</code> resets to <code>0x00</code> on any write and immediately starts counting again, so a sweep slow enough for the divider to tick reads nonzero there and has found a clock, not a mask.</div>' +

    '<p class="hw-readable"><b>A.6, the Super Game Boy packet protocol</b>, is in the same area, file <code>gb-sgb-emulation-tab.js</code> (<a href="#gbe-sgb">here</a>).</p></section>' +

  '</div>';
}
